#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186091946 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092327 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4eab94cb-5a5a-5915-ab95-963cb4c63817" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Closure Properties of Polynomials"}] :question/id #uuid "5ae8b304-5ce3-5520-8893-92a02784155d" :question/math-academy-id "e-1316" :question/problem "Which of the following expressions are polynomials?\n\nI. $(5x^2-10x^3) \\div (5x)$\n\nII. $(1-x^3) \\div (x^3)$\n\nIII. $(2x^5) \\cdot (-x^3-7)$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that polynomials have the following closure properties:\n\n- Polynomials are closed under addition, subtraction, and multiplication. This means that the sum, difference, and product of two polynomials is *always* a polynomial.\n\n- Polynomials are *not* closed under division. This means that the quotient of two polynomials is *not* always a polynomial.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our expressions in turn.\n\n- Expression I is a polynomial. Even though polynomials are *not* closed under division, the quotient of these two polynomials results in a polynomial:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(5{x}^{2}-10{x}^{3})÷(5x) & =\\frac{5{x}^{2}-10{x}^{3}}{5x} \\\\  & =\\frac{5x(x-2{x}^{2})}{5x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{5x}(x-2{x}^{2})}{\\cancel{5x}} \\\\  & =x-2{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Expression II is *not* a polynomial. Carrying out the division, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1-{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}} & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is *not* a polynomial.\n\n- Expression III is a polynomial. It's a product of two polynomials, and polynomials are closed under multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186091947 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092328 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "36318568-6dfe-5ba9-b607-a2d269d05425" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Constraints on the Degree of a Polynomial"}] :question/id #uuid "53557831-390f-5b8b-b5c3-6634d425c04d" :question/math-academy-id "e-1317" :question/problem "If $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are polynomials of degree $3$ and $2$, respectively, what is the degree of $P - Q?$" :question/worked-solution "Since $P$ is a polynomial of degree $3,$ the leading term contains an $x^3.$\n\nSince $Q$ is a polynomial of degree $2,$ the leading term contains an $x^2.$\n\nIn $P-Q,$ the highest-exponent term still contains an $x^3.$ There is no $x^3$ term in $Q$ that could possibly cancel out the $x^3$ term in $P.$\n\nTherefore, the degree of $P-Q$ must be $3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186091948 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092329 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2a0cc5ea-7f70-5c5f-841e-01a6813da8c6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Operations on Polynomials"}] :question/id #uuid "621d4d9b-ebfb-56ef-9614-807077aea4c3" :question/math-academy-id "e-1318" :question/problem "Given that $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are polynomials of degree $5$, which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $P \\cdot Q$ is always a polynomial of degree $10$,\n\nII. $P + Q$ is always a polynomial of degree $5$,\n\nIII. $\\dfrac{P}{Q}$ is sometimes a polynomial." :question/worked-solution "Let's look at each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The highest-degree terms in $P$ and $Q$ must both contain $x^5.$ Therefore, the highest-degree term in $P \\cdot Q$ must contain $x^5 \\cdot x^5 = x^{5+5} = x^{10},$ meaning that $P \\cdot Q$ has degree $10.$\n\n- Statement II is false. For example, suppose that $P=x^5$ and $Q=-x^5.$ Then $P+Q = x^5+(-x^5)=0,$ which is not a polynomial of degree $5.$\n\n- Statement III is true. For example, suppose that $P=x^5$ and $Q=x^5.$ Then $\\dfrac{P}{Q} = \\dfrac{x^5}{x^5} = 1,$ which is a polynomial of degree $0.$\n\nIn conclusion, only statements I and III are true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092159 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092312 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5f497212-3946-51e1-9c39-45284ab55da6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Degree of a Product of Polynomials"}] :question/id #uuid "d0f6fda4-4083-5541-8f0e-9a61f740ba8b" :question/math-academy-id "e-9981" :question/problem "If $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are polynomials of degree $2$ and $8$, respectively, what is the degree of $P \\cdot Q?$" :question/worked-solution "Since $P$ is a polynomial of degree $2,$ the leading term contains an $x^2.$\n\nSince $Q$ is a polynomial of degree $8,$ the leading term contains an $x^8.$\n\nIn $P \\cdot Q,$ the $x^2$ term in $P$ multiplies with the $x^8$ term in $Q$ to form a term containing $x^2 \\cdot x^8 = x^{2+8} = x^{10}.$\n\nTherefore, the degree of $P \\cdot Q$ must be $10.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186525045 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092328 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "36318568-6dfe-5ba9-b607-a2d269d05425" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Constraints on the Degree of a Polynomial"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c361adb-343e-5c84-8601-16635b6b133f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524403} {:answer/id #uuid "0e444044-682b-5424-9061-f93957c72200" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524407} {:answer/id #uuid "9739ff6f-a1af-5aad-8988-1ec0ed7516e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524622} {:answer/id #uuid "dd0a6baa-fc11-5a1b-a2dc-e92ce0f3cb70" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524757} {:answer/id #uuid "f4261bdc-6742-54b0-ab0c-020429c3f663" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524813}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dd0a6baa-fc11-5a1b-a2dc-e92ce0f3cb70" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524757} :answer-field/id #uuid "47f10e55-2a14-563d-841d-335a4383d49b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524870}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d4519b02-5082-5dd4-add0-c15242596fca" :question/math-academy-id "q-111384" :question/problem "If $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are polynomials of degree $6$, then what is the **smallest possible** degree of $P-Q?$" :question/worked-solution "The smallest possible degree of $P-Q$ is $0.$\n\nFor example, suppose $P={x}^{6}$ and $Q={x}^{6}+1.$ Then $P-Q=-1,$ which is a polynomial of degree $0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524980 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092329 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2a0cc5ea-7f70-5c5f-841e-01a6813da8c6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Operations on Polynomials"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "013b2dbc-18f3-55e3-9565-85cf066921b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524379} {:answer/id #uuid "0b628a86-1b5b-5261-81fe-b8ec5b9b6d66" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524402} {:answer/id #uuid "59b6ab9a-09a2-534d-bb1d-5e67fdedfdd7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524536} {:answer/id #uuid "82a41674-bf89-54e4-b672-208eafd52df6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524584} {:answer/id #uuid "b2492f6d-535d-5780-9d80-a92596e9f6a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524669}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "82a41674-bf89-54e4-b672-208eafd52df6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524584} :answer-field/id #uuid "317c380e-b8ef-5ea3-b2f2-20c14b4d4859" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524860}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "40dff619-4373-5ec6-a963-8c0ddd65024c" :question/math-academy-id "q-111387" :question/problem "If $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are polynomials of degree $3$ and $7,$ respectively, which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $P+Q$ is a polynomial of degree $3$\n\nII. $P-Q$ is always a polynomial\n\nIII. $\\frac{2{x}^{5}\\cdot P}{Q}$ is sometimes a polynomial" :question/worked-solution "Let's look at each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. The highest-degree terms in $P$ and $Q$ must contain ${x}^{3}$ and ${x}^{7},$ respectively. Since there is no ${x}^{7}$ term in $P$ that could possibly cancel the ${x}^{7}$ term in $Q,$ the leading term in $P+Q$ still contains ${x}^{7}.$ Therefore, the degree of $P+Q$ must always be $7.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Polynomials are closed under subtraction.\n\n- Statement III is true. For example, suppose $P={x}^{3}$ and $Q={x}^{7}.$ Then, $\\frac{2{x}^{5}\\cdot P}{Q}=\\frac{2{x}^{5}\\cdot {x}^{3}}{{x}^{7}}=2x,$ which is a polynomial.\n\nIn conclusion, only statements II and III are true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186525034 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092327 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4eab94cb-5a5a-5915-ab95-963cb4c63817" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Closure Properties of Polynomials"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3bd31b5f-4edb-5614-90b1-3db842e42842" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524491} {:answer/id #uuid "62b3d276-65bb-589c-bef8-28434c7b3a4e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524544} {:answer/id #uuid "731874e9-843e-5b25-9236-a231f2d5a98f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524566} {:answer/id #uuid "73340f47-6707-588d-91fe-a6db909018b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524567} {:answer/id #uuid "a3288bcb-72e7-5d83-920c-df3c72f026c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524642}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "73340f47-6707-588d-91fe-a6db909018b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524567} :answer-field/id #uuid "8f34e0d0-1b01-548e-8ace-ce5ffc57a3fe" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524902}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "bc096998-81ea-5954-87f7-a6c387f0821d" :question/math-academy-id "q-141233" :question/problem "Which of the following expressions are polynomials?\n\nI. $({x}^{2}-3x)+({x}^{3})$\n\nII. $(1-2x)-(2-{x}^{2})$\n\nIII. $(x+1)\\cdot (4-{x}^{2})$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that polynomials have the following closure properties:\n\n- Polynomials are closed under addition, subtraction, and multiplication. This means that the sum, difference, and product of two polynomials is *always* a polynomial.\n\n- Polynomials are *not* closed under division. This means that the quotient of two polynomials is *not* always a polynomial.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our expressions in turn.\n\n- Expression I is a polynomial. It's a sum of two polynomials, and polynomials are closed under addition.\n\n- Expression II is a polynomial. It's a difference between two polynomials, and polynomials are closed under subtraction.\n\n- Expression III gives a polynomial. It's a product of two polynomials, and polynomials are closed under multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186525003 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092327 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4eab94cb-5a5a-5915-ab95-963cb4c63817" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Closure Properties of Polynomials"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f14a0e5-b515-5250-8a1b-eb814cf89647" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524438} {:answer/id #uuid "5e906c3e-515c-5c94-8ce1-0dfb67aa41c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524539} {:answer/id #uuid "6a67d87d-b061-5ffd-9986-887ce52f98ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524555} {:answer/id #uuid "d2074c51-9d9b-528e-97bf-67ff6b78335c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524731} {:answer/id #uuid "db4123da-0e66-5dc6-b2de-212a6e29b246" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524751}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1f14a0e5-b515-5250-8a1b-eb814cf89647" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524438} :answer-field/id #uuid "68aa1c15-4097-51da-a3fa-04695a76160c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524887}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "83c42558-b7ba-54b4-8258-155dbb7534d4" :question/math-academy-id "q-141239" :question/problem "Which of the following expressions are polynomials?\n\nI. $(3{x}^{4})\\cdot ({x}^{3}-2x)$\n\nII. $(5x)+(-{x}^{7}-8)$\n\nIII. $(3{x}^{3}-{x}^{2}-1)÷({x}^{3})$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that polynomials have the following closure properties:\n\n- Polynomials are closed under addition, subtraction, and multiplication. This means that the sum, difference, and product of two polynomials is *always* a polynomial.\n\n- Polynomials are *not* closed under division. This means that the quotient of two polynomials is *not* always a polynomial.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our expressions in turn.\n\n- Expression I gives a polynomial. It's a product of two polynomials, and polynomials are closed under multiplication.\n\n- Expression II is a polynomial. It's a sum of two polynomials, and polynomials are closed under addition.\n\n- Expression III is *not* a polynomial. Carrying out the division, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3{x}^{3}-{x}^{2}-1}{{x}^{3}} & =\\frac{3{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{3}}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}} \\\\  & =3-\\frac{1}{x}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is *not* a polynomial.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186525010 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092312 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5f497212-3946-51e1-9c39-45284ab55da6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Degree of a Product of Polynomials"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d36b948-45e9-522f-aa03-f7e65100fc67" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524435} {:answer/id #uuid "315f6f41-73d5-5a7c-9f30-bd9a8163e36d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524473} {:answer/id #uuid "48c504b3-5dbd-5772-b6de-1051007f0040" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524507} {:answer/id #uuid "676adfbb-a0fb-55bc-b2d0-8fa166d87f74" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524551} {:answer/id #uuid "f419ec20-70f8-5eeb-b096-e9b51c1355f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524812}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "315f6f41-73d5-5a7c-9f30-bd9a8163e36d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524473} :answer-field/id #uuid "e7b0c143-901f-594e-996e-d3db3b783f1d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524942}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8fd741b4-3eaf-54db-b2d4-05c07c61f572" :question/math-academy-id "q-141253" :question/problem "If $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are polynomials of degree $3$ and $5$, respectively, what is the degree of $P\\cdot Q?$" :question/worked-solution "Since $P$ is a polynomial of degree $3,$ the leading term contains an ${x}^{3}.$\n\nSince $Q$ is a polynomial of degree $5,$ the leading term contains an ${x}^{5}.$\n\nIn $P\\cdot Q,$ the ${x}^{3}$ term in $P$ multiplies with the ${x}^{5}$ term in $Q$ to form a term containing ${x}^{3}\\cdot {x}^{5}={x}^{3+5}={x}^{8}.$\n\nTherefore, the degree of $P\\cdot Q$ must be $8.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186525054 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092328 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "36318568-6dfe-5ba9-b607-a2d269d05425" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Constraints on the Degree of a Polynomial"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04cfb9d2-2cd0-5712-b30e-ce6cf00a4c92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524386} {:answer/id #uuid "2348e195-30db-5d7f-a0e6-010a69c22521" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524445} {:answer/id #uuid "3e0da817-2175-5afa-ae23-a7f075ad5147" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524498} {:answer/id #uuid "865ed96e-2f21-5df9-8914-9923d592ed7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524589} {:answer/id #uuid "bbe0bbef-b70c-5b78-9300-508da745c3b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524682}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3e0da817-2175-5afa-ae23-a7f075ad5147" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524498} :answer-field/id #uuid "97fda8ea-7946-555e-8e3d-7c1f8f5cb679" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524909}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e218d969-ef9b-5d39-ab4e-db60fd086832" :question/math-academy-id "q-141264" :question/problem "If $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are polynomials of degree $4$ and $5$, respectively, what is the degree of $P+Q?$" :question/worked-solution "Since $P$ is a polynomial of degree $4,$ the leading term contains an ${x}^{4}.$\n\nSince $Q$ is a polynomial of degree $5,$ the leading term contains an ${x}^{5}.$\n\nIn $P+Q,$ the highest-order term still contains an ${x}^{5}.$ No ${x}^{5}$ term in $P$ could possibly cancel out the ${x}^{5}$ term in $Q.$\n\nTherefore, the degree of $P+Q$ must be $5.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524998 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092328 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "36318568-6dfe-5ba9-b607-a2d269d05425" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Constraints on the Degree of a Polynomial"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "88292a8a-7973-5f2a-9503-7a6937881e83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524594}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "88292a8a-7973-5f2a-9503-7a6937881e83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524594} :answer-field/id #uuid "fd91f76b-a516-5ea2-bf7f-fa1bf44a8403" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524952}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "78e65e3d-79b6-5ea5-b7c8-3423e62535bc" :question/math-academy-id "q-269924" :question/problem "If $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are polynomials of degree $11$ and $10$, respectively, the degree of $P+Q$ is{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "Since $P$ is a polynomial of degree $11,$ the leading term contains an ${x}^{11}.$\n\nSince $Q$ is a polynomial of degree $10,$ the leading term contains an ${x}^{10}.$\n\nIn $P+Q,$ the highest-order term still contains an ${x}^{11}.$ No ${x}^{11}$ term in $P$ could possibly cancel out the ${x}^{11}$ term in $Q.$\n\nTherefore, the degree of $P+Q$ must be $11.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524975 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092312 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5f497212-3946-51e1-9c39-45284ab55da6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Degree of a Product of Polynomials"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "25ab30e4-25f8-5a00-8578-49e889f2c8d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524451}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "25ab30e4-25f8-5a00-8578-49e889f2c8d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524451} :answer-field/id #uuid "ce49d847-d598-5aa9-ac7a-bc9bb0b2cb3b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524929}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "33175368-8c02-5445-a715-949176f366cb" :question/math-academy-id "q-277891" :question/problem "If $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are polynomials of degree $3$ and $12$, respectively,  the degree of $P\\cdot Q$ is{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "Since $P$ is a polynomial of degree $3,$ the leading term contains an ${x}^{3}.$\n\nSince $Q$ is a polynomial of degree $12,$ the leading term contains an ${x}^{12}.$\n\nIn $P\\cdot Q,$ the ${x}^{3}$ term in $P$ multiplies with the ${x}^{12}$ term in $Q$ to form a term containing ${x}^{3}\\cdot {x}^{12}={x}^{3+12}={x}^{15}.$\n\nTherefore, the degree of $P\\cdot Q$ must be $15.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092712 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092328 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "36318568-6dfe-5ba9-b607-a2d269d05425" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Constraints on the Degree of a Polynomial"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a44f84ca-9e0f-579d-a632-df9e6439aae4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090473} {:answer/id #uuid "c1a3e181-9a28-540a-a8b7-4dff4