/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524447} {:answer/id #uuid "5175a5b8-1b67-52c5-bb4f-d2031c4d90f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524520} {:answer/id #uuid "b3d95f9f-44bf-5ba3-ac57-4899559d619a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524671} {:answer/id #uuid "c7ce5e7a-fe12-56de-9ac6-5f857ea15a0a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524705} {:answer/id #uuid "e3905f1a-6f12-51e1-81b5-1c33222125ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524780}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c7ce5e7a-fe12-56de-9ac6-5f857ea15a0a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524705} :answer-field/id #uuid "6c813721-2908-5a92-be17-13c7cb6f3034" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524889}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "617b4238-abb7-5b3b-bc81-af12e3c18aa5" :question/math-academy-id "q-51541" :question/problem "Given that $P(x)$ is a polynomial of degree $4,$ which of the following statements are always true?\n\nI. $\\frac{{x}^{4}}{P(x)}$ is a polynomial\n\nII. ${x}^{4}\\cdot P(x)$ is a polynomial of degree $8$\n\nIII. ${x}^{4}+P(x)$ is a polynomial of degree $4$" :question/worked-solution "Let's look at each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. For example, suppose $P={x}^{4}+2.$ Then $\\frac{{x}^{4}}{P(x)}=\\frac{{x}^{4}}{{x}^{4}+2},$ which is not a polynomial.\n\n- Statement II is true. The highest-degree term in $P$ must contain ${x}^{4}.$ Therefore, the highest-degree term in ${x}^{4}\\cdot P(x)$ must contain ${x}^{4}\\cdot {x}^{4}={x}^{4+4}={x}^{8},$ meaning that ${x}^{4}\\cdot P(x)$ has degree $8.$\n\n- Statement III is false. For example, suppose $P=-{x}^{4}.$ Then ${x}^{4}+P(x)={x}^{4}-{x}^{4}=0,$ which is not a polynomial of degree $4.$\n\nIn conclusion, only statement II is true."}]}
